ny required check fails, unclear if any is unresolved,\notherwise upheld. Copy broken words from the requirement into violates.\n\n\n## B08.1\n\n**Check:** Network policy or security groups default-deny and allow only the listed systems and data stores the agent needs.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find NetworkPolicy, security groups, firewall rules, docker-compose networks. 2) Check for a default deny and named allowed peers. 3) Answer no if the agent's namespace has no policy, rules allow all, `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad CIDR is allowed, or allowed peers go beyond the listed systems. Answer unknown if network config lives outside this repo.\n\n**Coverage policy:** {}\n\n**Candidate sites (trace aliases and missed paths manually):** []\n\n## B08.2\n\n**Check:** No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants.\n\n**Coverage policy:** {}\n\n**Candidate sites (trace aliases and missed paths manually):** []\n\n## B08.3\n\n**Check:** Isolation is enforced in infrastructure config, not by the prompt or by agent code the model can influence.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find where isolation is defined. 2) Check it is in infrastructure (NetworkPolicy, VPC, IAM, separate namespace or account). 3) Answer no if it relies on prompt text or agent code (URL checks, host lists in the harness) that the model's output can steer, with no infrastructure rule behind it.\n\n**Coverage policy:** {}\n\n**Candidate sites (trace aliases and missed paths manually):** []\n\n## Your answer\n\nWrite this JSON to B08.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"B08\"):\n```json\n{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B08.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\", \"coverage\": [], \"deviations\": [], \"attempts\": []}],\n \"applicability\": \"absent | present | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\",\n \"note\": \"one sentence\"}\n```\nVerdict is **failed** if any check fails, **unclear** if none fails but any is unclear, otherwise **upheld**. A failed check needs the quoted line that makes it false: `evidence` is path:line, and `quote` is the exact text of that line. To cite two places, list both refs in `evidence` (`a.py:12, b.yaml:40`) and put each line's text on its own line in `quote`. If the check fails, add `\"violates\": [\"words copied exactly from the requirement\"]`.\nagent\nagent/migrations\nagent/migrations/001_init.sql\nagent/requirements.txt\nagent/config\nagent/config/agent.yaml\nagent/config/tools.yaml\nagent/Dockerfile\nagent/k8s\nagent/k8s/deployment.yaml\nagent/k8s/audit-maintenance.yaml\nagent/k8s/admission.yaml\nagent/k8s/networkpolicy.yaml\nagent/CODEOWNERS\nagent/.mcp.json\nagent/release\nagent/release/manifest.yaml\nagent/README.md\nagent/.github\nagent/.github/workflows\nagent/.github/workflows/release.yml\nagent/prompts\nagent/prompts/system.md\nagent/src\nagent/src/killswitch.py\nagent/src/gateway_client.py\nagent/src/gateway.py\nagent/src/audit.py\nagent/src/approvals.py\nagent/src/credentials.py\nagent/src/tools.py\nagent/src/type_id.py\nagent/src/redact.py\nagent/src/retrieval.py\nagent/src/agent.py\nagent/src/budgets.py\nagent/src/main.py\nagent/src/validation.py","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_